  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nVery high surface moisture content Saturday afternoon coming down \nslowly overnight with most dewpoints below 80 degrees at 06Z. With \n850mb temperatures in the mid 20s C and good insolation pushing \nsurface temps to the mid to upper 90s, most places saw apparent \ntemps over 115 at least briefly. Mean guidance was too low with \ndewpoints Saturday, and values closer to the 75th percentile of NBM \nwere realized. Though there should be somewhat better mixing today \nand ground conditions continue to dry, went closer to the 75th \npercentile dewpoints for today. In combination with slightly warmer \ntemps off the surface, apparent temps should be around Warning \nlevels this afternoon, and given the high-end conditions Saturday, \nwill go ahead with a Warning area-wide today. Most guidance suggests \nthe warmest air temps may come Monday though also bring dewpoints \ndown as low-level flow veers and increases somewhat. This should \nallow for slightly lower apparent temps, still in Advisory levels \nfor the bulk of the area. \n\nStill appears the upper ridge over the Southwest weakens somewhat \nlate Monday and allows a weak cold front to push through Monday \nnight into Tuesday. Precipitation chances remain modest but should \nincrease later in the week as stronger shortwave energy rounds the \nridge and moves through the northern and central Plains. Tuesday \ncould still bring notable heat to at least southern areas depending \non how fast the front passes, dewpoints behind it, and any \nconvection, so the Extreme Heat Watch seems reasonable to keep at \nthis point. The pattern supports generally cooler, closer to normal \nconditions Wednesday into Saturday.",
